    "title": "Hall Thruster Surrogate Benchmark",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22758006",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-14",
    "description": "<p># Hall thruster surrogate benchmark</p>\n<p>This repository holds the code that generated a dataset of 16,800 Hall thruster<br>plasma simulations (a 12,000-run core set plus two 2,400-run extrapolation<br>pools), and the evaluation protocol that turns that dataset into a benchmark. A<br>Hall thruster is an electric rocket engine used on spacecraft, and<br>simulating one takes about twenty seconds per operating point, which is fast for<br>plasma physics and far too slow for anything that needs thousands of evaluations.<br>The usual answer is to train a machine learning model that imitates the<br>simulator. The hard question is not whether such a model fits its training data,<br>but how it behaves away from it, and that question is normally answered with a<br>single random train/test split that hides the answer. This benchmark replaces<br>that with seven splits of the same data, each one asking a different and<br>generally harder version of \"what happens away from the training points\":<br>ordinary interpolation, interpolation with less training data, extrapolation past<br>the edge of the sampled range in two different directions, and holes cut out of<br>the middle of it. The splits are fixed and published as data files, and the<br>scoring rule is one short function, so two people evaluating two models are<br>measuring the same thing.</p>\n<p>Companion dataset: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21986782</p>\n<p>Source snapshot of release v1.0.0 of the GitHub repository.</p>",
